      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Practice short questions for the whole of Unit 10 (Trigonometric Identities), arranged by the unit's sections, in the style of a 2-mark Board short question. Each one is solved briefly, step by step."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.1 Distance Formula: (Recall)"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "1 Find the distance between the points \\((-1, -2)\\) and \\((5, 6)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(d = \\sqrt{(5 - (-1))^{2} + (6 - (-2))^{2}} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "2fda6d2a-30f6-52ed-9d24-b9755b183e47",
              "quote": "For this we need the formula for the distance between two points in a plane."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "2 The distance between \\((1, k)\\) and \\((4, 5)\\) is \\(5\\). Find the possible values of \\(k\\).",
            "explanation": "\\((4 - 1)^{2} + (5 - k)^{2} = 25 \\Rightarrow (5 - k)^{2} = 16 \\Rightarrow 5 - k = \\pm 4\\), so \\(k = 1\\) or \\(k = 9\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "2fda6d2a-30f6-52ed-9d24-b9755b183e47",
              "quote": "The order of subtraction does not matter, because the differences are squared."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "3 Find the distance between \\((2\\cos\\theta, 0)\\) and \\((0, 2\\sin\\theta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(d = \\sqrt{4\\cos^{2}\\theta + 4\\sin^{2}\\theta} = \\sqrt{4(\\cos^{2}\\theta + \\sin^{2}\\theta)} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "2fda6d2a-30f6-52ed-9d24-b9755b183e47",
              "quote": "The coordinates may be numbers or trigonometric expressions; the method is the same."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "4 Show that \\(A(0, 0)\\), \\(B(3, 4)\\) and \\(C(6, 0)\\) are the vertices of an isosceles triangle.",
            "explanation": "\\(AB = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5\\), \\(BC = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5\\), \\(AC = 6\\). Two sides are equal, so the triangle is isosceles.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "2fda6d2a-30f6-52ed-9d24-b9755b183e47",
              "quote": "Taking the root of each term separately"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.1.1 Fundamental Law of Trigonometry"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "5 Verify the fundamental law \\(\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta + \\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta\\) for \\(\\alpha = 90^{\\circ}\\), \\(\\beta = 60^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "L.H.S. \\(= \\cos 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). R.H.S. \\(= 0\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} + 1\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Both sides are equal.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "7cf30dd5-7135-533a-9e0b-677a40244f90",
              "quote": "which is called the Fundamental Law of Trigonometry."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "6 If \\(\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{8}{17}\\), \\(\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{15}{17}\\), \\(\\sin\\beta = \\frac{4}{5}\\) and \\(\\cos\\beta = \\frac{3}{5}\\), find \\(\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\frac{15}{17}\\cdot\\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{8}{17}\\cdot\\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{45 + 32}{85} = \\frac{77}{85}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "7cf30dd5-7135-533a-9e0b-677a40244f90",
              "quote": "Although we have proved this law for"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "7 Write \\(\\cos 2\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\sin 2\\theta\\sin\\theta\\) as a single trigonometric ratio.",
            "explanation": "It has the form \\(\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta + \\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta = \\cos(\\alpha - \\beta)\\) with \\(\\alpha = 2\\theta\\), \\(\\beta = \\theta\\): \\(\\cos(2\\theta - \\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "7cf30dd5-7135-533a-9e0b-677a40244f90",
              "quote": "3. Pairing the wrong ratios: it is"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "8 Show by one example that \\(\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) \\ne \\cos\\alpha - \\cos\\beta\\) in general.",
            "explanation": "Take \\(\\alpha = 60^{\\circ}\\), \\(\\beta = 0^{\\circ}\\): \\(\\cos 60^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), but \\(\\cos 60^{\\circ} - \\cos 0^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2} - 1 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\). They are not equal.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "7cf30dd5-7135-533a-9e0b-677a40244f90",
              "quote": "Writing \\(\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\cos\\alpha - \\cos\\beta\\)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.1.2 Deductions from Fundamental Law"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "9 If \\(\\tan\\alpha = 2\\) and \\(\\tan\\beta = 3\\), find \\(\\tan(\\alpha + \\beta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\tan(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\dfrac{2 + 3}{1 - 2\\cdot 3} = \\dfrac{5}{-5} = -1\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "Why divide by \\(\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta\\)? Because"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "10 If \\(\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{3}{5}\\), \\(\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{4}{5}\\), \\(\\sin\\beta = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\cos\\beta = \\frac{12}{13}\\), find \\(\\sin(\\alpha - \\beta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot\\frac{12}{13} - \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot\\frac{5}{13} = \\frac{36 - 20}{65} = \\frac{16}{65}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "for sine the sign follows the bracket"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "11 Prove that \\(\\dfrac{\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)}{\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta} = \\tan\\alpha + \\tan\\beta\\).",
            "explanation": "L.H.S. \\(= \\dfrac{\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta + \\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta}{\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\cos\\alpha} + \\dfrac{\\sin\\beta}{\\cos\\beta} = \\tan\\alpha + \\tan\\beta\\) = R.H.S.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "From the single law of 10.1.1 the book now deduces ten results"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "12 Without tables, find \\(\\sin 165^{\\circ}\\) by writing \\(165^{\\circ} = 120^{\\circ} + 45^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin 165^{\\circ} = \\sin 120^{\\circ}\\cos 45^{\\circ} + \\cos 120^{\\circ}\\sin 45^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "Quick use of (ix): \\(\\tan 75^{\\circ} = \\tan(45^{\\circ} + 30^{\\circ}) = \\frac{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}{1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} = 2 + \\sqrt{3}\\)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "13 Prove that \\(\\cos(60^{\\circ} + \\theta) + \\cos(60^{\\circ} - \\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\).",
            "explanation": "L.H.S. \\(= (\\cos 60^{\\circ}\\cos\\theta - \\sin 60^{\\circ}\\sin\\theta) + (\\cos 60^{\\circ}\\cos\\theta + \\sin 60^{\\circ}\\sin\\theta) = 2\\cos 60^{\\circ}\\cos\\theta = 2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta = \\cos\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "changing the sign inside the bracket changes the sign in the middle"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "14 Simplify \\(\\sin(30^{\\circ} + \\theta) - \\cos(60^{\\circ} + \\theta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\sin\\theta\\right) - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\sin\\theta\\right) = \\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "97d91995-25cc-5c97-ab9e-b5da4ba7d191",
              "quote": "Putting \\(\\beta = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) in it, we get"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.2 Trigonometric Ratios of Allied Angles"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "15 Without tables, find \\(\\sin 510^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(510^{\\circ} = 5 \\times 90^{\\circ} + 60^{\\circ}\\): odd, so sin becomes cos. \\(510^{\\circ}\\) ends at \\(150^{\\circ}\\) (quadrant II) where sin is positive: \\(\\sin 510^{\\circ} = \\cos 60^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "If \\(n\\) is even the ratio stays the same; if \\(n\\) is odd it changes to its co-ratio."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "16 Without tables, find \\(\\tan(-600^{\\circ})\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\tan(-600^{\\circ}) = -\\tan 600^{\\circ}\\). \\(600^{\\circ} = 6 \\times 90^{\\circ} + 60^{\\circ}\\): even, tan stays; it ends at \\(240^{\\circ}\\) (quadrant III), tan positive: \\(\\tan 600^{\\circ} = \\sqrt{3}\\). So \\(\\tan(-600^{\\circ}) = -\\sqrt{3}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "For a negative angle, take the minus out first"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "17 Without tables, find \\(\\sec 870^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(870^{\\circ} = 9 \\times 90^{\\circ} + 60^{\\circ}\\): odd, sec becomes cosec. It ends at \\(150^{\\circ}\\) (quadrant II) where sec is negative: \\(-\\operatorname{cosec} 60^{\\circ} = -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "The above results also apply to the reciprocals of sine, cosine and tangent."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "18 Express \\(\\cos 205^{\\circ}\\) as a trigonometric ratio of a positive angle less than \\(45^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(205^{\\circ} = 180^{\\circ} + 25^{\\circ}\\) (even multiple, quadrant III, cos negative): \\(\\cos 205^{\\circ} = -\\cos 25^{\\circ}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "the ratios of a large angle can be found from those of an acute angle"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "19 Express \\(\\tan 296^{\\circ}\\) as a trigonometric ratio of a positive angle less than \\(45^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(296^{\\circ} = 270^{\\circ} + 26^{\\circ}\\): odd multiple, tan becomes cot; quadrant IV, tan negative: \\(\\tan 296^{\\circ} = -\\cot 26^{\\circ}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "the trigonometric ratios change into co-ratios and vice versa"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "20 Prove that \\(\\sin(90^{\\circ} + \\theta)\\cos(180^{\\circ} - \\theta) = -\\cos^{2}\\theta\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin(90^{\\circ} + \\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\) (quadrant II, sin positive) and \\(\\cos(180^{\\circ} - \\theta) = -\\cos\\theta\\) (quadrant II, cos negative). Product \\(= -\\cos^{2}\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "The sign of the result is decided by the quadrant in which the terminal arm of the angle lies."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "21 Prove that \\(\\tan(180^{\\circ} - \\theta) + \\tan(360^{\\circ} - \\theta) + 2\\tan\\theta = 0\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\tan(180^{\\circ} - \\theta) = -\\tan\\theta\\) (quadrant II) and \\(\\tan(360^{\\circ} - \\theta) = -\\tan\\theta\\) (quadrant IV). L.H.S. \\(= -\\tan\\theta - \\tan\\theta + 2\\tan\\theta = 0\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "c2d1fa49-1e2f-51e2-8a26-c33e75354c37",
              "quote": "If \\(\\theta\\) is added to or subtracted from an even multiple of"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.3 Further Application of Basic Identities"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "22 Using \\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)\\sin(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\sin^{2}\\alpha - \\sin^{2}\\beta\\), find the value of \\(\\sin^{2}75^{\\circ} - \\sin^{2}15^{\\circ}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin^{2}75^{\\circ} - \\sin^{2}15^{\\circ} = \\sin 90^{\\circ}\\sin 60^{\\circ} = 1\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "Remember this result as a ready-made identity"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "23 Show that \\(\\dfrac{1 + \\tan 15^{\\circ}}{1 - \\tan 15^{\\circ}} = \\sqrt{3}\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\dfrac{\\tan 45^{\\circ} + \\tan 15^{\\circ}}{1 - \\tan 45^{\\circ}\\tan 15^{\\circ}} = \\tan(45^{\\circ} + 15^{\\circ}) = \\tan 60^{\\circ} = \\sqrt{3}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "appears often. In the same way"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "24 If \\(\\cos\\alpha = -\\frac{5}{13}\\) and the terminal side of \\(\\alpha\\) is in quadrant II, find \\(\\sin\\alpha\\) and \\(\\tan\\alpha\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin\\alpha = \\pm\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{25}{169}} = \\pm\\frac{12}{13}\\); positive in quadrant II: \\(\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{12}{13}\\), \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{12/13}{-5/13} = -\\frac{12}{5}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "choose each sign from the quadrant"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "25 \\(\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{3}{5}\\) with \\(\\alpha\\) in quadrant II and \\(\\cos\\beta = -\\frac{12}{13}\\) with \\(\\beta\\) in quadrant III. Find \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\cos\\alpha = -\\frac{4}{5}\\), \\(\\sin\\beta = -\\frac{5}{13}\\). \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right) - \\frac{3}{5}\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right) = \\frac{48 + 15}{65} = \\frac{63}{65}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "The square root gives two signs and the quadrant chooses one."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "26 With the data of the previous question, find \\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)\\) and name the quadrant of \\(\\alpha + \\beta\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\frac{3}{5}\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right) = \\frac{-36 + 20}{65} = -\\frac{16}{65}\\). Sine negative and cosine \\(\\frac{63}{65}\\) positive: quadrant IV.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "quadrant IV is the only quadrant where cosine is positive and sine is negative"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "27 If \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\) are the angles of a triangle, prove that \\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)\\cos\\gamma + \\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\sin\\gamma = 0\\).",
            "explanation": "L.H.S. has the form \\(\\sin A\\cos B + \\cos A\\sin B = \\sin(A + B)\\): it is \\(\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = \\sin 180^{\\circ} = 0\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "In a triangle \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 180^{\\circ}\\)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "28 Express \\(20\\sin\\theta + 21\\cos\\theta\\) in the form \\(r\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)\\), with \\(\\phi\\) in quadrant I.",
            "explanation": "\\(r\\cos\\phi = 20\\), \\(r\\sin\\phi = 21\\): \\(r = \\sqrt{400 + 441} = \\sqrt{841} = 29\\), \\(\\tan\\phi = \\frac{21}{20}\\). So \\(20\\sin\\theta + 21\\cos\\theta = 29\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)\\) with \\(\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\frac{21}{20}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "\\(a\\sin\\theta + b\\cos\\theta = r\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)\\), \\(r = \\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}\\)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "29 Express \\(\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta\\) in the form \\(r\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)\\) and give \\(\\phi\\) in degrees.",
            "explanation": "\\(r\\cos\\phi = \\sqrt{3}\\), \\(r\\sin\\phi = 1\\): \\(r = \\sqrt{3 + 1} = 2\\), \\(\\tan\\phi = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), \\(\\phi = 30^{\\circ}\\). So it is \\(2\\sin(\\theta + 30^{\\circ})\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "b4e67428-a9dc-5340-a982-f61de0a0da34",
              "quote": "Because \\(-1 \\le \\sin(\\theta + \\phi) \\le 1\\)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "10.4 Double Angle Identities"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "30 If \\(\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{8}{17}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) is acute, find \\(\\sin 2\\alpha\\) and \\(\\cos 2\\alpha\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{15}{17}\\). \\(\\sin 2\\alpha = 2\\cdot\\frac{15}{17}\\cdot\\frac{8}{17} = \\frac{240}{289}\\); \\(\\cos 2\\alpha = 2\\cdot\\frac{64}{289} - 1 = -\\frac{161}{289}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "cedc2356-1c48-59e0-a83a-38321640675a",
              "quote": "Choose the one that contains what is given or what you need"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "31 If \\(\\tan\\alpha = 2\\), find \\(\\tan 2\\alpha\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(\\tan 2\\alpha = \\dfrac{2\\cdot 2}{1 - 4} = -\\dfrac{4}{3}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "cedc2356-1c48-59e0-a83a-38321640675a",
              "quote": "The tangent formula works only when"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "32 Prove that \\(\\dfrac{1 - \\cos 2\\theta}{1 + \\cos 2\\theta} = \\tan^{2}\\theta\\).",
            "explanation": "\\(1 - \\cos 2\\theta = 2\\sin^{2}\\theta\\) and \\(1 + \\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^{2}\\theta\\), so L.H.S. \\(= \\dfrac{2\\sin^{2}\\theta}{2\\cos^{2}\\theta} = \\tan^{2}\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "cedc2356-1c48-59e0-a83a-38321640675a",
              "quote": "Rearranging the last two forms gives two very useful results"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "self_check",
            "prompt": "33 Prove that \\((\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta)^{2} = 1 + \\sin 2\\theta\\).",
            "explanation": "L.H.S. \\(= \\sin^{2}\\theta + \\cos^{2}\\theta + 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = 1 + \\sin 2\\theta\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "cedc2356-1c48-59e0-a83a-38321640675a",
              "quote": "we only have to put \\(\\beta = \\alpha\\) in the sum formulas"
            }
          },
          {
